#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""pal (also crew): how a Project Pals pal (or you) works with the rest of the team.

The team
  pal overview [--deep]             everything at once: pals, topics (what each is on), projects
                                     (git), memory, tasks, rooms, routines, approvals, what's stuck
  pal team                          the short roster
  pal feed [--all] [-n 40]          what happened since you last looked (finished, blocked, stuck…)
  pal peek <Pal[#topic]> [-n 60]    read a pal's screen and recent history, and what it's waiting on if blocked
                                     (a prompt and its options: only the owner answers those, in its tab)

Talking
  pal tell <Pal[#topic]> <text> [--now]
                                     message a pal; delivered when its turn ends (--now: right away,
                                     for a pal that's stuck mid-turn). You hear back when it finishes.
  pal ask <Pal[#topic]> <text> [--timeout 600]
                                     message a pal, wait for its answer, print it
  pal room ...                      group rooms (pal-room --help); pal invite <room> <Pal[#topic]>...

The Desk (everything sent to you is a card with a status and receipts)
  pal desk [ls] [--all|--route|--owner|--held] [--done] [--json]
                                     your open cards (this topic + your pal tray); --all: everyone's
  pal desk open ID                  the whole card (marks it seen · opened)
  pal desk take ID [--sub] | later ID | done ID ["result"] | reply ID "answer"
  pal desk block ID "why" [--owner] | pass ID <Pal[#topic]> "why"
  pal desk send <Pal[#topic]|owner|route|auto> "text" [--soon|--low] [--ask] [--task] [--attach F]... [-F file]
            [--relaying] [--re ID]   a new card (pal tell / ask / task add --for send cards too)
  pal desk sent [--json] | cancel ID | attach FILE | stats | check
  pal desk draft --to <Pal#topic|auto|route> [--urgency U] [--task] [--attach F]... -F draft.md
                                     the idea partner's draft: it waits for the owner's Send (ls --drafts)
  (the owner, from Project Pals: urgency ID LEVEL, confirm ID, send-draft ID [--to …], discard ID, prune --older 180d)

Pop out (the owner, from Project Pals: a topic's live terminal in its own desktop window)
  pal popout ls                     the pop-outs that are open
  (the owner's click: popout <Pal[#topic]>, popout pin KEY, popout close KEY. The window floats, can be
   pinned to every workspace (Hyprland), resizes freely, and remembers its size, place and pin.)

Home (the head pal keeps it current: what needs the owner, links, what's queued; the app shows it)
  pal home [--json] [--factory NAME|all]   what's on Home (with several factories: the one you're in)
  pal home ask "question" [--option A [--does "what A does"]]... [--recommend A --why "…"] [--topic P#t]
            [--body …] [--link URL]... [--image FILE]...   a decision for the owner (options: one click each)
  pal home recommend <a-…|c-…|h-…> <option> --why "…"   your pick for an approval (approve|decline),
                                     a Desk card (done|open) or an ask (one of its options)
  pal home login "! npx vercel login" --topic P#t [--title …]       a login the owner runs in that topic
  pal home link URL [--title …] [--topic P#t] | note "one line" | deploy "what" [--topic P#t]
  pal home queue ["what"] [--release 0.4.1] [--note "…"] | unqueue ID   the next release and what's in it
  pal home done ID ["result"] | park ID|"title" | unpark ID | drop ID
  pal welcome                       where the welcome tour stands (it runs on Home; replay it from the gear menu)
  pal welcome folders [--json]      the project folders the tour would suggest for a new pal (names and dates only)
  pal factory [ls|show NAME] [--json]   the factories: crews with their own foreman and Home. Your
                                    crew is the default one; until you make another, nothing changes
  pal factory new NAME [--template project-crew|platform-lanes] [--foreman Pal] [--foreman-topic T]
  pal factory switch NAME | move PAL NAME | foreman NAME [PAL] [--topic T] | rename NAME NEW | rm NAME
                                    (changes are the owner's: a click in Project Pals)
  (the owner, from Project Pals: answer ID "option", seen)

Hiring
  pal hire <Name> [--harness claude|agy|…] [--model M] [--role R] [--topic general] [--folder DIR] [--machine M] [--task T]
  pal hire --partner [Name]         the idea partner (MUSE by default): you riff, it drafts cards you Send
  (the owner, from Project Pals: hire --chief [Name], the Chief: a pop-up chief of staff that only reads
   and hands work to the head pal's #ops; Ctrl+Shift+J)
  pal topic <Pal> <name> [--brief T] [--category C] [--harness H] [--model M] [--area A] [--worktree [--base BRANCH]]
                                     a new topic (a tab); --worktree gives it its own git branch and folder;
                                     --area files it under one of the pal's areas (made if new).
  pal topic area ls [Pal] [--json] | add <Pal> <Area> | set <Pal#topic> <Area|none> | rm <Pal> <Area> | rename <Pal> <Area> <New>
                                     areas: a pal's topics filed under a few names, for listing only.
                                     Pal#topic stays the address; an area is never part of it.
                                     pal channel is the same command (topics were called channels before 0.4.0)
  pal sleep [<Pal[#topic]>... | #topic | --idle] [-n]
                                     Sleep (keep topics, free RAM): stop a pal's agent; its tabs and room seats
                                     stay. Refuses one that's working, asking, being typed to or running work.
                                     (Close pal, in the app, ends its space instead.)
  pal wake <Pal[#topic]|#topic>... [-n]   Wake (resume topics): its brain again in the same tab, resuming
                                     that topic's session when it can vouch for it, else fresh (it says why)

Work
  pal task add "<title>" [--for Pal[#topic]] [--after T1,T2] [--body T] [--check "cmd"] [--due fri]
  pal task ls [--all] | ready | show T | claim T | note T "…" | block T "why" | done T ["result"]
            | assign T Pal | cancel T | reopen T
  pal task due T <when|none>         when: fri, tomorrow, 2026-11-14, +3d, fri 17:00 (a pal: its own tasks)
  pal computer [Pal] [today|todo|calendar|journal|pages] [--json] [--start D] [--days N]
                                     what a pal's Computer shows (read-only)
  pal page ls [Pal] | show "<title>" [--pal P] | write "<title>" -F file|- | rm "<title>"
                                     its freeform pages (a pal writes its own), in its Computer's Notes
  pal routine add <Pal> "<when>" "<prompt>" [--name N] [--topic routines]
                                     when: weekdays 09:00, every 2h 10:00-20:00, mon,fri 4pm, or cron
  pal routine ls | log [-n 20] | run ID | pause ID | resume ID | rm ID
  pal routine edit ID [--name N] [--when "…"] [--prompt "…"] [--topic C]
  pal diff <Pal#topic> [--stat|--json] what a worktree topic changed
  pal land <Pal#topic> [--check "cmd"] merge a worktree topic's branch into its project (needs the owner)

Mission, brain and agent files (the profile panel shows them all)
  pal mission [Pal] [--json]        a pal's mission: what it's for, goals, focus, done when
  pal mission focus "…" | tick "goal" [--undo]          keep your focus current, tick a goal
  pal mission propose -F draft.md   the mission is the owner's: propose one, it goes to the Inbox
  pal brain [ls] [Pal] [--json] | show [Pal] [file]     the pal's brain (mission, decisions, change log, map, notes)
  pal brain log "what changed" | decide "what" --why "why" | note <subject> "…"
  pal agents show|lint [Pal] [--json] [--deep]          the project's AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md: what loads, what to fix
  pal agents propose <file> -F new.md [--note "why"]    a change to AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md, for the owner to apply
  pal proposals | pal proposal show|accept|decline ID   proposals waiting on the owner (accept/decline: the owner)

Notebook (how the work went; the Dream loop learns from it)
  pal notebook "did: …" [--hiccup "what got in the way (cost: ~15m)"]… [--needed "skill: …"]… [--lesson "…"] [--task T] [--took 40m]
  pal notebook hiccup "…" [--needed "tool: …"]    a quick one, mid-work
  pal notebook [ls] [Pal] [--days 7] [--json]   today's (ls: the last days)
                                     needed kinds: tool, skill, rule, context, access. Never secrets,
                                     private data or pasted output (masked or refused)

Dream loop (the crew learns from its notebooks; proposals only, the owner approves each)
  pal dream [--dry-run] [--no-model] [--force]   group the notebooks' hiccups into patterns; qualifying
                                     ones go to the head pal as a digest (nightly at 03:30 by crewd too)
  pal dream ls [--json]             patterns, proposals and how they turned out
  pal dream propose --pattern P-n --kind skill|rule|tool|process|context [--name N] [--pal P] -F f --note "why"
  pal dream intake --pattern P-n -F note.md     a sanitized draft for DJM OS's intake (the owner files it)

Memory and skills
  pal remember "<fact>" [--user]    save a durable fact for your future sessions (--user: about the owner)
  pal memory [Pal] [--json]         what's remembered (--json: what a session starts with, for the profile panel)
  pal skill ls | show NAME | save NAME --description "…" [--file F, else stdin] | archive NAME

Settings
  pal settings                      who the pals work for, head pal, bypass, and background LLM
  pal settings owner "Name" | head "Name" | head-topic ops|none | bypass on|off | llm auto|claude|agy
  pal settings desk | desk.<key> <value>   the Desk's settings (triage, caps, flags, archive, MUSE)

Updates
  pal version                       what's installed
  pal update [check|apply|rollback] [--json]
                                     check GitHub for a newer release, install it, or put the previous one back
  pal uninstall [--purge] [--dry-run] [--json]
                                     take Project Pals out (the owner's: About → Uninstall): everything it added,
                                     nothing of yours; pals' data stays unless --purge (--dry-run: just list it)

Approvals (the owner)
  pal approvals                     guarded commands waiting on the owner
  pal approve ID | pal deny ID ["note"]

"Pal" matches a pal's name or the start of it ("docs" finds "Docs Site"); without
#topic you get the one it's showing (Pal#topic: a pal's tabs are its topics; --channel and
pal channel still work, as before 0.4.0). hire, topic and invite are carried out by the Project Pals layout,
so Herdr has to be open on it. crewd (herdr-crew.service) delivers messages between turns, runs
routines and reports back; without it, messages are typed in right away.
"""
